package pane:extension@0.1.0;

// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 OR MIT
//
// Background work: a command's scheduled task, which Pane runs every so
// often in the background once the user turns its schedule on in Manage
// extensions. Kept apart from extension.wit so a command without one is
// unchanged: only a command whose `pane.json` entry declares a `schedule`
// (`"schedule": { "everyMinutes": 15 }`) exports this interface, beside
// `command`. Rust commands export it through pane-guest
// (`pane_guest::scheduled::export!`), JS/TS commands by exporting
// `scheduledTask` with `"scheduledTask": true` in their package.json's
// `"pane"` options.
// The header follows `package`: comments above it would document the package,
// which extension.wit already does.

interface scheduled-task {
  /// Runs the scheduled task of the command `command` (its id in
  /// `pane.json`, so one component can serve several commands) once. The
  /// returned text is shown in Manage extensions as the task's latest
  /// result; an error is shown as its latest failure, and is an ordinary
  /// outcome, never a crash: the schedule goes on.
  ///
  /// Pane runs it in the background, in an instance of its own started for
  /// this run and dropped after it, so nothing kept in memory survives
  /// between runs: keep what the next run needs in extension data. It runs
  /// no more than once at a time. It is stopped where it waits (and code
  /// after that `await` never runs) when the package is disabled, reloaded,
  /// updated, paused or uninstalled, or the user turns its schedule off.
  /// Wait on async imports (a clock, a web request) rather than computing
  /// for long without yielding: Pane serves every extension's calls on one
  /// thread, which a task that never yields holds. A task cannot call
  /// other packages' operations yet: such a call is refused.
  run-task: async func(command: string) -> result<string, string>;
}

/// The scheduled task export alone: a command component with a schedule
/// exports it besides the `extension` world's `command`.
world scheduled-task-provider {
  export scheduled-task;
}
